{"title": "Foundations of Information Retrieval", "learningObjectives": [
    "Define the core components of a search engine architecture, including indexing, retrieval, and ranking.",
    "Explain how traditional statistical models like TF-IDF and BM25 compute document relevance scores.",
    "Apply a practical analysis checklist to evaluate search system performance using precision, recall, and NDCG."
  ], "beats": [
    {
      "title": "Search Engine Architecture Overview",
      "claim": "Search engines organize digital documents into structured indexes to enable fast, relevant query responses.",
      "example": "A web crawler indexes HTML pages by extracting text, building an inverted index, and storing term frequencies.",
      "visualDirection": "Display a flowchart showing query input, index lookup, and result output with labeled components.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-536"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Query Processing and Parsing",
      "claim": "User queries are parsed and transformed into structured representations before index traversal.",
      "example": "A natural language query like 'climate change effects' is tokenized and normalized to match indexed terms.",
      "visualDirection": "Show a side-by-side comparison of raw query text versus parsed query components with highlighted tokens.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-536"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Inverted Index Construction",
      "claim": "Inverted indexes map terms to document IDs, enabling efficient retrieval of relevant documents.",
      "example": "The term 'machine' points to document IDs [101, 105, 112], allowing direct access to containing pages.",
      "visualDirection": "Render a simplified inverted index table with term columns and document ID lists.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-536"]
    },
    {
      "title": "TF-IDF Relevance Scoring",
      "claim": "TF-IDF calculates document relevance by weighting term frequency against inverse document frequency.",
      "example": "A term appearing in many documents receives a lower IDF weight, reducing its relevance score.",
      "visualDirection": "Illustrate TF-IDF calculation with a small document corpus showing term frequencies and IDF values.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-536"]
    },
    {
      "title": "BM25 Ranking Algorithm",
      "claim": "BM25 refines TF-IDF by incorporating document length normalization and position weighting.",
      "example": "Short documents receive higher scores for rare terms, while BM25 adjusts scores based on term position in text.",
      "visualDirection": "Display a comparison table of TF-IDF versus BM25 scores for the same query across different documents.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-536"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Neural Reranking Techniques",
      "claim": "Neural reranking models re-evaluate top-ranked results using deep learning representations.",
      "example": "A transformer-based model scores query-document pairs by analyzing semantic similarity in high-dimensional space.",
      "visualDirection": "Show a neural network diagram with query and document embeddings merging into a relevance score output.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-536"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Search Evaluation Metrics",
      "claim": "Search performance is measured using precision, recall, and NDCG to quantify relevance quality.",
      "example": "Precision measures the fraction of retrieved documents that are relevant, while NDCG accounts for ranking order.",
      "visualDirection": "Present a precision-recall curve and NDCG graph with labeled axes and example points.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-536"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Conversational and Enterprise Search",
      "claim": "Modern search systems support multi-turn dialogue and domain-specific enterprise queries.",
      "example": "Enterprise search indexes internal documents with role-based access and contextual filters for business users.",
      "visualDirection": "Show a dialogue flow for conversational search and a dashboard for enterprise search filters.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-536"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Experimental Design in IR",
      "claim": "Search engine components are evaluated through controlled experiments comparing retrieval and ranking methods.",
      "example": "A group project tests BM25 versus neural reranking on a benchmark corpus to measure score improvements.",
      "visualDirection": "Display an experimental setup diagram with baseline, variant, and evaluation metrics.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-536"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Practical Analysis Checklist",
      "claim": "A structured checklist ensures systematic evaluation of search system performance and relevance.",
      "example": "The checklist verifies index coverage, ranking consistency, metric alignment, and error cost assessment.",
      "visualDirection": "Show a printable checklist template with checkboxes for each evaluation criterion.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-536"]
    }
  ]}
